course: dictionary
lesson: 5325
title: Context clues: kerosene
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Kerosene is a hard word, but the words around it help. «In 1853 Ignacy Łukasiewicz made a kerosene lamp in Lviv, and it lit a hospital operation.» A lamp gives light, so kerosene must be something a lamp can burn to make light.
explain: Look for more clues. Kerosene is a thin, clear oil that is burned as fuel in lamps, heaters, stoves and jet engines. A fuel is something you burn to get heat or power. So kerosene is a liquid fuel that burns.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. Does «a kerosene lamp» make sense as «an oil that burns lamp»? It does, so your meaning fits. Remember that places can use different names: in some countries, kerosene is called paraffin.
fact: Kerosene is a thin, clear oil made from petroleum.
fact: Kerosene is burned as fuel in lamps, heaters, stoves and jet engines.
fact: Jet fuel is mostly kerosene, so planes use it to fly.
fact: In Australia, the USA and Canada, kerosene is the usual name for paraffin oil; in the UK it is often called paraffin.
fact: Kerosene is flammable, so keep it away from children and flames.
exercise: «In 1853 Ignacy Łukasiewicz made a kerosene lamp in Lviv.» What is a kerosene lamp? | Lamp that runs on sunshine alone | Lamp made of ice | *Lamp that burns a thin, clear oil to give light | Lamp that is lit by a thin, clear oil
exercise: Planes run on jet fuel, which is mostly kerosene. Where is kerosene burned in a plane? | In the windows | In the seats | In the wheels | *In the jet engines
exercise: Grandma in Canada says, «Fill the kerosene for the heater.» Her friend in London calls it something else. What might the London friend call kerosene? | *Paraffin | Petrol for cars | Lemonade | Milk
test: What is a kerosene stove? | Stove made of ice | Stove that makes cold air | *Stove that burns a thin, clear oil as fuel | Stove that runs on sunshine
test: Why must kerosene be kept away from children and flames? | Because it smells of roses | *Because it is flammable and can catch fire | Because it is too cold to touch | Because it turns into sugar
exam: Kerosene is burned as fuel in lamps and jet engines. In the USA it is the usual name, but in the UK it is often called what? | Petrol | Water | Ink | *Paraffin
